In article <cpnrk2$3gt$1@joe.rice.edu>, Andy Johnson
<usenet@take_this_out.swervy.tk> wrote:

> There's a riding spoiler for vanilla (and probably applies to [S]), but 
> it's more about what your chances are to saddle and mount a steed. I 
> don't know of any others.
> 
> All of this info is for vanilla. I've heard nothing about any 
> differences in [S]. You probably have a better choice of steed.

I'd seen that spoiler, and it really didn't explain why one would bother
having a steed.

> There are a lot of benefits to steeds, and some downsides. You get the 
> speed of your steed, so if you get a very fast steed (unicorns, 
> warhorses have twice base speed, ki-rin and jabberwocks can be very 
> fast). You can kick a steed to make it gallop (temporary speed boost) 
> but it loses some tameness. For carnivores, tameness isn't a problem 
> because they eat everything.
> 
> You can fly if your steed can. I don't think you can jump a horse, never 
> tried though. Once you get to basic in riding, you don't need to 
> dismount to pick up items. Basic comes after moving 2000 spaces while 
> riding, which is not so bad.
> 
> Steeds will take some damage for you as the enemy attacks them instead 
> of you. They will do some damage (but only if attacked first, 
> unfortunately).
> 
> You can't wear conflict while riding. You can't levitate your steed. 
> I've never tried water walking. You can teleport and levelport with the 
> steed. Saddles can be hard to come by if you don't start with one. You 
> can't eat corpses off the ground while riding, but you can still pick 
> them up and eat them if you're at least Basic (possible bug?).
> 
> My favorite steed is a Jabberwock. They're fast, powerful, carnivorous, 
> and not too rare. Warhorses are faster and easier to get in the 
> beginning though, and if it goes feral it's easy to retame.

The above info seems like at least a *start* for a possible spoiler - I
would never have thought to *try* riding a jabberwock, for instance.

But in a way, it raises the issue of why not ask the DevTeam to put a
little more energy into the whole steed thing. For instance:

-magic saddle (weighs very little, no untaming effect from mounting repeatedly)

-maybe a magic whistle variant that teleports your steed into position
under you (though I suppose this would require that a steed be coded as
something other than a pet, which might not be desirable)

-magic feedbag (produces nutritious horse chow like HoP)

-having a #jump option if it doesn't already exist; maybe requiring at
least basic skill

-having a lengthened hunger timeout when riding, if it doesn't already exist

-having a weight-sharing effect (might be a little tricky to program, but,
logically, having your steed carry heavy weights you couldn't manage by
yourself is pretty much what having one SHOULD be about). Every game I've
ever played I play more than 95% of the game either "burdened" or
"stressed", even WITH a blessed BoH, and +5GoS. If a pet Archon can carry
a sword and shield, why couldn't a steed carry an item called "saddlebags"
which you can loot while mounted? It'd be nice to be able to *carry*
things like crystal balls, ice boxes, tinning kits, and spellbooks instead
of having to drop them because they're simply too heavy (i.e., when your
blessed BoH already weighs 1200 units). Wouldn't it be nice to be able to
retrieve and *use* some of those land mines in Fort Ludios, in addition to
the gold?

-having certain critters that only certain classes/races/alignments can
use, like allowing gnomes to ride some relatively small monsters, or
letting only celibate females ride unicorns, or letting only flame mages
ride red dragons, etc.

-aside from some things like mumaks, mastodons, and baluchitheria which
might already be allowed, adding a few new steed types (the "u" monster
class really doesn't have much in it) like hippocampi or hippopotami on
some watery special levels, for water travel, or a skeletal horse for
necromancers

-maybe taking that idea even further and having some class-specific "Steed
Quests" that give a unique, special steed to the character, and a bonus
for ascending with said steed

You get the idea...there are many ways that steeds could be improved upon
so they really represent something more than just another novelty pet. At
*least* consider the weight-sharing thing. While I can certainly imagine
that there are purists who look at the 1000 unit weight limit as an
integral and important part of the game, and managing one's inventory
within those limits to be part of the role-playing, but it's always struck
me as a little inconsistent, since most of the fantasy literature, even
D&D, is realistic enough to allow beasts to carry stuff *for* the heroes.

